    "subject_name": "Physics",
    "chapter_id": "616023db0dd81950d11cd0c2",
    "chapter_name": "Mechanical Properties of Fluids",
    "year": "2022",
    "shift": "shift_1"
  },
  "question": {
    "text": "The diameter of an air bubble which was initially <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>, rises steadily through a solution of density $1750 \\mathrm{~kg} / \\mathrm{m}^3$ at the rate of $0.35 \\mathrm{~cm} / \\mathrm{s}$. Coefficient of viscosity of the solution is ______ (Assume mass of the bubble to be negligible) (Answer in Poise to the nearest integer)"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "11",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>As the bubble is rising steadily the net force acting on it will be zero.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0085_c0aa7bdfd59b.png\" style=\"width: 120px; height: 141px;\" /></p><p>(Because of density of air is negligible, so the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi></math> can be neglected)</p><p>So, the force of buoyancy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> is equal to the weight of the displaced liquid acting upward is equal to the viscous force acting downward, i.e.,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>&#951;</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>v</mi></math></p><p>Putting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#961;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>75</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>35</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#951;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mn>9</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#183;</mo><mn>11</mn></math></p><p>In&nbsp;SI unit,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#951;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></math>&nbsp;poise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>CGS</mi></mfenced></math>.</p></blockquote>"
  }
}